---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import InShort from '../../components/article/InShort.astro';
import ZenTip from '../../components/article/ZenTip.astro';
import ZenPicture from '../../components/article/ZenPicture.astro';

const VOSS = 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0118308';
const SHAFFER = 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5624990/';
const HEALTHKIT_SDNN = 'https://developer.apple.com/documentation/healthkit/hkquantitytypeidentifier/heartratevariabilitysdnn';

// Voss et al. 2015, PLOS ONE, Tables 5 (women) and 7 (men): mean ± SD, ms.
const hrvByAge = [
  { age: '25–34', sdnnF: '48.7 ± 19.0', sdnnM: '50.0 ± 20.9', rmssdF: '42.9 ± 22.8', rmssdM: '39.7 ± 19.9', nF: 208, nM: 330 },
  { age: '35–44', sdnnF: '45.4 ± 20.5', sdnnM: '44.6 ± 16.8', rmssdF: '35.4 ± 18.5', rmssdM: '32.0 ± 16.5', nF: 259, nM: 292 },
  { age: '45–54', sdnnF: '36.9 ± 13.8', sdnnM: '36.8 ± 14.6', rmssdF: '26.3 ± 13.6', rmssdM: '23.0 ± 10.9', nF: 158, nM: 235 },
  { age: '55–64', sdnnF: '30.6 ± 12.4', sdnnM: '32.8 ± 14.7', rmssdF: '21.4 ± 11.9', rmssdM: '19.9 ± 11.1', nF: 95, nM: 183 },
  { age: '65–74', sdnnF: '27.8 ± 11.8', sdnnM: '29.6 ± 13.2', rmssdF: '19.1 ± 11.8', rmssdM: '19.1 ± 10.7', nF: 62, nM: 84 },
];

// Answers repeat the opening paragraphs under each matching H2 below.
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is a normal HRV for my age?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "There is no single normal HRV range for everyone of the same age. Published averages go down with age, but people of the same age vary a lot. A useful comparison has to match the metric, how long the recording was and how it was taken. A quick Apple Watch reading, a 5-minute resting ECG and a 24-hour ECG are not the same thing." } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is RMSSD?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'RMSSD stands for root mean square of successive differences between normal heartbeats. It looks at how much each gap between beats differs from the next one, so it shows short-term, beat-to-beat change. It is measured in milliseconds.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is SDNN?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'SDNN is the standard deviation of normal-to-normal (NN) beat intervals. It describes the overall spread of the gaps between beats during a recording, measured in milliseconds. It is the HRV metric Apple Health stores for Apple Watch.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Can I compare my Apple Watch HRV with an HRV-by-age chart?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Only loosely. Apple Watch records SDNN, so a chart's SDNN columns are the closest match. But the Watch takes automatic samples while you are still, not a 5-minute ECG lying down after a rest. Use a chart to see the general age trend, and mainly compare your Watch readings with your own history." } },
  ],
};
---
<ArticleLayout
  title="Normal HRV by Age: Chart, RMSSD, SDNN & Apple Watch"
  description="An HRV-by-age chart from a 1,906-person study, plain definitions of RMSSD and SDNN, and why Apple Watch HRV can't be compared one-to-one with published norms."
  updated="2026-11-12"
  schema={faqSchema}
>
<InShort points={[
  'There is <strong>no single normal HRV</strong> for your age. People vary a lot.',
  'Average HRV <strong>goes down with age</strong> in large studies.',
  'Compare your Apple Watch HRV <strong>with your own history</strong> first.',
]} />

<h2>What is a normal HRV for my age?</h2>
<p><strong>There is no single normal HRV range for everyone of the same age.</strong> Published averages go down with age, but people of the same age vary a lot.</p>
<p>A useful comparison has to match the metric, how long the recording was and how it was taken. A quick Apple Watch reading, a 5-minute resting ECG and a 24-hour ECG are not the same thing.</p>
<p>Heart rate variability (HRV) is how much the time between your heartbeats changes. <a href={SHAFFER}>Shaffer and Ginsberg's review of HRV metrics and norms</a> explains why age, recording length and the people studied all affect "normal" values. It warns that 24-hour, short-term and ultra-short-term norms can't be swapped for each other.</p>
<p>Patterns across a big group of people don't give you a cut-off for diagnosing your own Watch reading.</p>

<h2 id="hrv-by-age-chart">HRV by age chart (SDNN and RMSSD)</h2>
<p><strong>The table shows averages from one large study of healthy adults.</strong> Each value is the average ± standard deviation, in milliseconds (ms).</p>
<p>The standard deviations are big. That shows how much healthy people of the same age differ.</p>
<div class="not-prose -mx-1 my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)]" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="HRV by age table, scrolls sideways">
  <table class="w-full min-w-[34rem] text-sm text-left">
    <caption class="sr-only">Short-term HRV by age and sex, Voss et al. 2015</caption>
    <thead class="bg-[color:var(--color-mint-soft)] text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">
      <tr>
        <th scope="col" rowspan="2" class="px-3 py-2 font-bold align-bottom">Age</th>
        <th scope="colgroup" colspan="2" class="px-3 pt-2 font-bold">SDNN (ms)</th>
        <th scope="colgroup" colspan="2" class="px-3 pt-2 font-bold">RMSSD (ms)</th>
      </tr>
      <tr class="text-xs text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)]">
        <th scope="col" class="px-3 pb-2 font-semibold">Women</th>
        <th scope="col" class="px-3 pb-2 font-semibold">Men</th>
        <th scope="col" class="px-3 pb-2 font-semibold">Women</th>
        <th scope="col" class="px-3 pb-2 font-semibold">Men</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-[color:var(--color-border)] text-[color:var(--color-ink)] tabular-nums">
      {hrvByAge.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-2.5 font-bold whitespace-nowrap">{r.age}</th>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 whitespace-nowrap">{r.sdnnF}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 whitespace-nowrap">{r.sdnnM}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 whitespace-nowrap">{r.rmssdF}</td>
          <td class="px-3 py-2.5 whitespace-nowrap">{r.rmssdM}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)]"><strong>Source:</strong> <a href={VOSS}>Voss et al., “Short-Term Heart Rate Variability — Influence of Gender and Age in Healthy Subjects,” PLOS ONE, 2015</a>, Tables 5 and 7. <strong>Measurement:</strong> SDNN and RMSSD from 5-minute ECG recordings taken lying down (supine) after 5–10 minutes of rest. <strong>Population:</strong> 1,906 healthy adults aged 25–74 (782 women, 1,124 men) from the KORA S4 cohort in the Augsburg region of Germany, after excluding people with arrhythmias, documented diseases, heart-rate-affecting medication or pregnancy. Group sizes range from 62 (women 65–74) to 330 (men 25–34). The study did not include people under 25 or over 74.</p>
<p><strong>What the chart shows:</strong> in this group, both SDNN and RMSSD were lower in each older age group up to the mid-60s. The study found no significant difference between the two oldest groups (55–64 and 65–74).</p>
<p>Women and men had similar average SDNN and RMSSD in every age group.</p>
<p>Treat the numbers as a picture of one group of people, not a pass or fail range.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_presenting_new.webp">
  Use the chart to see the <strong>trend</strong>, not to grade yourself. Your own readings over time tell you more.
</ZenTip>

<h2 id="apple-watch-hrv-by-age">Apple Watch HRV by age: can I compare it with a chart?</h2>
<p><strong>Only loosely. Apple Watch records SDNN, so a chart's SDNN columns are the closest match.</strong> But the Watch takes automatic samples while you are still, not a 5-minute ECG lying down after a rest.</p>
<p>Use a chart to see the general age trend, and mainly compare your Watch readings with your own history.</p>
<p>Apple's <a href={HEALTHKIT_SDNN}>HealthKit documentation</a> says HealthKit uses SDNN, the standard deviation of the gaps between normal heartbeats. It also says Apple Watch records samples automatically. Apple's <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">heart-rate support page</a> adds that HRV is measured while you're still, so the number of readings changes with how active you are.</p>
<p>That's why a Watch reading can't be lined up exactly against the table:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Different sensor.</strong> The study used an ECG. The Watch uses its light-based (optical) heart sensor for background readings.</li>
  <li><strong>Different recording.</strong> The study used a fixed 5-minute recording lying down after rest. Watch samples happen automatically, at different times of day and night, in whatever position you're in.</li>
  <li><strong>Recording length changes SDNN.</strong> The <a href={SHAFFER}>Shaffer and Ginsberg review</a> says SDNN is more accurate over 24 hours than over shorter periods. Short-term and 24-hour norms can't be swapped.</li>
  <li><strong>Different people.</strong> The study group was screened healthy adults aged 25–74 in one region of Germany.</li>
</ul>
<p>A better question than "is my number normal for my age?" is <strong>"how does this reading compare with my own readings taken in similar conditions?"</strong></p>

<ZenPicture src="/app-ui/start/zen_watch.webp" caption="Your best comparison is your own past readings." />

<h2 id="what-is-rmssd">What is RMSSD?</h2>
<p><strong>RMSSD stands for root mean square of successive differences between normal heartbeats.</strong> It looks at how much each gap between beats differs from the next one, so it shows short-term, beat-to-beat change. It is measured in milliseconds.</p>
<p>The <a href={SHAFFER}>Shaffer and Ginsberg review</a> calls RMSSD the main time-based measure used to estimate vagally mediated HRV changes (changes driven by the vagus nerve). It is more affected by the parasympathetic ("rest and digest") nervous system than SDNN is.</p>
<p>Apple Health stores SDNN, not RMSSD, for Apple Watch.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-sdnn">What is SDNN?</h2>
<p><strong>SDNN is the standard deviation of normal-to-normal (NN) beat intervals.</strong> It describes the overall spread of the gaps between beats during a recording, measured in milliseconds. It is the HRV metric Apple Health stores for Apple Watch.</p>
<p>SDNN picks up every kind of variation during the recording. So its value depends a lot on how long you record and in what conditions.</p>
<p>A 24-hour SDNN, a 5-minute resting SDNN and an automatic Watch sample are not the same thing. That's true even though all three are called SDNN and measured in milliseconds.</p>

<h2>Is SDNN the same as RMSSD?</h2>
<p><strong>No.</strong> SDNN describes the spread of the gaps between normal beats. RMSSD describes how much each gap differs from the next.</p>
<p>The <a href={SHAFFER}>review's metric definitions</a> explain the difference. The table above shows the two give different numbers for the same recordings.</p>
<p>Don't convert a number from one into the other just because both use milliseconds.</p>

<h2>Before using any HRV-by-age chart</h2>
<p><strong>Check that the chart matches how your reading was taken.</strong></p>
<div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4">
    <p class="font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Metric: SDNN or RMSSD</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Both use milliseconds, but they sum up beat-to-beat change in different ways.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4">
    <p class="font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Recording length</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">A short sample can't be judged against a 24-hour reference range.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4">
    <p class="font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Conditions</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Resting, exercising, sleeping and slow, paced breathing are all different situations.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4">
    <p class="font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Who was studied</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">A study's people and the way it measured them may be different from you.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>We don't give a universal "normal" number for a 30-, 40-, 50- or 60-year-old. The table above shows average short-term ECG values in one healthy group. It is not a tested reference range for Apple Watch readings.</p>

<h2>How do I check my Apple Watch HRV?</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Open Health on your iPhone and search for Heart Rate Variability.</li>
  <li>Look at the dates, times and data sources behind the values.</li>
  <li>Compare the same metric from the same source over similar periods.</li>
  <li>Note things like sleep, exercise and how you felt in a <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">simple observation journal</a>.</li>
  <li>If records are missing, follow the <a href="/blog/apple-watch-hrv-not-updating/">HRV troubleshooting checklist</a>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Apple describes what its heart sensor measures on <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">its heart-rate page</a>. To learn how sensor readings differ from calculated scores, read our <a href="/stress/apple-watch/">Apple Watch stress guide</a>.</p>

<a href="/start/" data-start-cta class="not-prose my-8 flex items-center gap-4 rounded-2xl bg-[color:var(--color-ink)] p-5 text-white no-underline shadow-sm transition hover:brightness-110">
  <img src="/apple-touch-icon.png" alt="" width="48" height="48" class="h-12 w-12 shrink-0 rounded-[13px]" />
  <span class="min-w-0 flex-1 leading-snug">
    <span class="block font-extrabold">Get your personal stress plan</span>
    <span class="block text-sm text-white/75">A one-minute check-in with Zen, then your plan in Cortisol+</span>
  </span>
  <span class="shrink-0 rounded-full bg-white px-4 py-2 text-sm font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Start ›</span>
</a>

<h2>Is a higher HRV always better?</h2>
<p><strong>No.</strong> The <a href={SHAFFER}>HRV norms review</a> warns that more variability isn't always a sign of better health.</p>
<p>A wearable reading alone can't tell you your health status or explain a change.</p>

<h2>What does Cortisol+ do with HRV?</h2>
<p><strong>Cortisol+ turns the wearable signals it can get into a wellness score and trend views.</strong> It starts from general population defaults. Once it has stored enough of your recent readings, it shifts toward a baseline built from your own data.</p>
<p>The baseline rules and how missing data is handled are in <a href="/how-it-works/">our methodology</a>. Cortisol+ does not measure cortisol or show that a hormone level went up or down.</p>
<p>Look at the input breakdown and the times, not just the summary score. To compare approaches before you download, use our <a href="/compare/apple-watch-stress-apps/">Apple Watch stress-app guide</a>.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_new_low.webp">
  Compare like with like: <strong>same watch, same metric, similar conditions</strong>. Then a change means more.
</ZenTip>

<h2>What if my number looks low?</h2>
<p><strong>A chart or app score can't tell you whether you need medical care.</strong></p>
<p>If you have symptoms or are worried about a change that lasts, talk to a qualified clinician about your readings and what was going on.</p>
<p>Don't use HRV to diagnose a cortisol disorder or to change a prescribed treatment.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
